      "question": {
        ":question/id": "39b99761-df6d-539c-9682-f60f84447665",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9 - 4 x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\frac{2 x}{3}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nFactor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{9 - 4 x^{2}}=3\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{2 x}{3})^2}.\n$$\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $1\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9 - 4 x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{3} \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
